# =============================================================================
# Trivy scan exceptions — IMAGES WE BUILD.        (policy: Phase 226 / 822)
# Consumed by `make scan-first-party` ONLY.
# =============================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS SEPARATE (Phase 822)
#   Until Phase 822 there was one `.trivyignore`, and the Makefile passed it to
#   BOTH the third-party and first-party scans. Trivy's ignorefile has no
#   per-image scoping, so every waiver granted to an abandoned monitoring
#   sidecar ALSO suppressed the same finding in the proxy we ship.
#
#   That was not hypothetical: CVE-2026-39821 and CVE-2026-46600 were waived
#   with justifications written entirely about Grafana, Promtail, Alertmanager
#   and cAdvisor — "they consume metrics/logs or serve authenticated admin UIs
#   only" — while both were live in ja4proxy:2.0.0, an internet-facing TLS
#   proxy. Both were fixed by a one-line Go bump (1.26.5 -> 1.26.6), which is
#   what Phase 822 did instead of renewing them.
#
# THE RULE HERE IS STRICTER THAN THE THIRD-PARTY FILE
#   We control these images. If a fix exists ANYWHERE upstream — a base-image
#   bump, a dependency bump, an apk/apt upgrade — it is not a waiver candidate.
#   Do the fix. An entry belongs here only when no rebuild we can perform
#   clears it (e.g. a library vendored inside pip itself).
#
#   Every entry still needs: a '#' block stating why no fix AND why not
#   exploitable here, plus an inline `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` at most 7 days out.
#
#   Verify with `make scan-exceptions`. Guide:
#   docs/runbooks/security_scan_exceptions.md
# =============================================================================


# protobuf 4.25.9 DoS (python: recursive parsing) in ja4proxy-test — the CI-only,
# profile-gated tooling image (never deployed; skipped by 'make build'). No fix
# available: protobuf 5.29.6 needs semgrep>=1.137, whose mcp==1.23.3 pin would
# reintroduce CVE-2025-66416 (see phase-800 note in requirements.txt). protobuf
# there is only exercised by semgrep/OTEL tooling on trusted repo content.
# Re-checked 2026-07-30 at semgrep 1.172.0 (latest on PyPI, well past the
# OTEL>=1.37 condition): opentelemetry-api/-sdk are now ~=1.37.0 as hoped, but
# semgrep still hard-pins mcp==1.23.3 — the actual blocker is unchanged.
# Re-review at next expiry: check again for a semgrep release with mcp>=1.28.
CVE-2026-0994 exp:2026-09-12

# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964: msgpack for Python out-of-bounds read/crash on malformed
# input (fixed in 1.2.1). Present in every first-party Python image.
# Why no fix (re-investigated 2026-08-06, Phase 818): this is NOT a dependency
# we install — `pip show msgpack` finds nothing. It is pip's OWN vendored copy:
# /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack (and a second
# copy inside the ensurepip bundled wheel). Even the latest pip — 26.2.1, which
# the images install via `pip install --upgrade pip` — still vendors msgpack
# 1.1.2. The requirements-level `msgpack>=1.2.1` pins are real and effective for
# any genuine top-level use, but they cannot touch pip's internal _vendor tree.
# pip (26.2.1, the current release, installed via `pip install --upgrade pip`)
# still vendors the old msgpack, so it is present in every image regardless of
# the base Python patch level. Only a future pip that re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.1
# clears this. The sibling CVE-2025-47273/setuptools is the same pip-vendored
# situation; both are exceptioned below.
# Why not exploitable: pip's vendored msgpack is used only by pip's own
# resolver/cache machinery at install time on trusted inputs; no application or
# runtime code path in any image imports it, and pip is not run at runtime.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-09-12

# CVE-2025-47273: setuptools path traversal in PackageIndex (fixed in 78.1.1).
# Same root cause as GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 above: NOT a dependency we install
# (`pip show setuptools` finds the live one is current) — this is pip's own
# vendored copy, pip/_vendor/pkg_resources, which Trivy reports as an old
# setuptools (~70.3.0). Present in pip 26.2.1's _vendor tree and the ensurepip
# bundled wheel. Re-investigated 2026-08-06 (Phase 818): the build is
# non-deterministic about whether Trivy surfaces this or the sibling msgpack
# CVE first, so both are exceptioned. Neither is fixable by us — only a future
# pip release that re-vendors these clears them.
# Why not exploitable: pip's vendored pkg_resources is used only by pip's own
# install-time machinery on trusted inputs; no application or runtime code path
# imports it, and pip is not run at runtime.
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-09-12

# CVE-2024-23342: python-ecdsa vulnerable to the Minerva timing side-channel
# attack (no fixed version — upstream ecdsa has no patch; the maintainer's
# stance is "constant-time signing is out of scope for this library").
# Why no fix: pulled in transitively by python-jose (management/requirements.txt)
# for JWT signing. The real remediation is migrating off python-jose to PyJWT
# (already tracked from phase-304 as a separate, larger auth-library change).
# Why not exploitable here: Minerva requires an attacker able to measure
# nanosecond-scale signing-time variance over many repeated signature
# operations against the same key — JA4proxy's JWT signing is HS256
# (HMAC, not ECDSA) by default; ecdsa is present only as python-jose's
# transitive dependency graph, not on the actual signing path used today.
# Phase 801 (ja4proxy-management Alpine rebase): the only residual finding
# after re-basing off python:3.14.6-alpine3.24 — expected per that phase's plan.
# Renewed 2026-07-30 (previous exp:2026-08-13 lapsed): re-checked upstream
# python-ecdsa — still no patched release, maintainer position unchanged.
# python-jose -> PyJWT migration (phase-304) still not started; nothing new
# to re-justify beyond re-confirming both facts above still hold.
CVE-2024-23342 exp:2026-09-12
